WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.280
So today, we're going to transition into the second module of this class.

00:10.280 --> 00:15.360
And in particular, we're going to talk about robotic perception.

00:15.360 --> 00:19.140
As you remember, the class is roughly divided into four modules.

00:19.140 --> 00:26.280
The first one is on trajectory optimization and optimal control, traditionally the hardest

00:26.280 --> 00:27.280
module.

00:27.280 --> 00:33.280
You should be happy that from now on, you will be downhill in terms of difficulty.

00:33.280 --> 00:40.280
Second module, robot perception, third module, localization and mapping, and finally, reasoning,

00:40.280 --> 00:44.080
state machines, stochastic decision making, and so on.

00:44.080 --> 00:49.340
In particular, for the next couple of weeks, as I mentioned, we will be covering robot

00:49.340 --> 00:54.200
perception, basically the process of gathering information about the environment and try

00:54.200 --> 01:02.500
to extract relevant features about the environment that allow the robot, on the one hand, to

01:02.500 --> 01:07.920
localize itself within the environment, and on the other hand, decide what is the best

01:07.920 --> 01:11.620
action to do next.

01:11.620 --> 01:16.680
So in this lecture today, first, I'm going to give you a broad overview of the sensors

01:16.680 --> 01:20.960
out there that the robot can use in order to perceive the environment.

01:20.960 --> 01:26.480
And then we're going to focus on a specific sensor that is of pivotal importance, which

01:26.480 --> 01:28.820
is the camera.

01:28.820 --> 01:35.920
And in particular, for most of this class, we'll be focusing either on the cameras or

01:35.920 --> 01:37.360
on ladders.

01:37.360 --> 01:45.260
And indeed, the robotics platform we'll be working with is both a laser trained sensor

01:45.260 --> 01:46.260
and a camera.

01:47.260 --> 01:53.220
So in the second part of this lecture, I'm going to give you an introduction to cameras,

01:53.220 --> 01:54.580
camera models.

01:54.580 --> 01:59.100
And then on Thursday, we're going to go deeper into cameras, and in particular, we're going

01:59.100 --> 02:05.880
to cover the topic of camera calibration and how to reconstruct the scene, and in particular,

02:05.880 --> 02:10.200
how to measure distances by using camera images.

02:10.200 --> 02:16.600
The following week, we will be then focusing on how to extract higher level information,

02:16.600 --> 02:23.920
such as geometrical properties of an environment, lines, walls, and so on, and also semantic

02:23.920 --> 02:29.860
information about environment, how do we detect whether there is a person, a cat, and so on.

02:29.860 --> 02:32.840
So let's start from the foundations.

02:32.840 --> 02:35.120
So here I'm showing you a self-driving car.

02:35.160 --> 02:40.640
A self-driving car is a prototypical autonomous system that showcases a number of sensors

02:40.640 --> 02:44.520
that are widely used in the field of robot autonomy.

02:44.520 --> 02:52.360
For example, a self-driving car has a number of cameras, has a ladder or multiple ladders,

02:52.360 --> 02:59.440
so laser sensors, as we will see next, to perceive the environment with high accuracy,

02:59.440 --> 03:12.000
as radars to perceive distant obstacles, ultrasonic sensors sometimes in order to perceive objects

03:12.000 --> 03:15.480
that are close by, GPS, and so on.

03:15.480 --> 03:20.880
So you can see how an autonomous robot is a machine that entails a number of very different

03:20.880 --> 03:24.200
sensors.

03:24.200 --> 03:29.320
Once you have the entire sensing pipeline set up and you already saw some of these images

03:29.320 --> 03:35.240
during the seminar of Jurg van der Berg during week one, that's how, and it's a video that's

03:35.240 --> 03:43.560
already showed you, that's how a prototypical robot abstracts the environment.

03:43.560 --> 03:47.000
So for example, this is the case of a self-driving car that is driving on the highway.

03:47.000 --> 03:50.040
This is the image from a camera.

03:50.040 --> 03:55.880
From the point of view of the robot, that's how the world looks like.

03:55.880 --> 04:02.920
You have bounding boxes representing the objects that are perceived.

04:02.920 --> 04:11.320
So everything gets, oops, there is some text here, some commentary.

04:11.320 --> 04:12.720
By the way, so you already saw this video.

04:12.720 --> 04:19.160
So the idea is to go from raw measurements all the way to semantic understanding.

04:19.160 --> 04:23.800
This is really the goal of robot perception.

04:23.800 --> 04:28.840
Now, the sensors can be classified in many different ways.

04:28.840 --> 04:33.720
One fundamental way is whether the sensors are proprioceptive or extraceptive.

04:33.720 --> 04:39.400
A proprioceptive sensor is a sensor that measures values that are internal to the robot.

04:39.400 --> 04:44.920
Like, for example, the speed of the motor or the position of the joint angles for a

04:44.920 --> 04:47.920
robotic arm and so on and so forth.

04:47.920 --> 04:53.760
In contrast, an extraceptive sensor is a sensor that acquires information from the robot's

04:53.760 --> 04:54.760
environment.

04:54.760 --> 05:01.760
Like, for example, distance measurements acquired through a ladder.

05:01.760 --> 05:06.400
Another important distinction for sensors is whether they are passive or active.

05:06.400 --> 05:12.160
A passive sensor is a sensor that measures energy in the environment, say, for example,

05:12.160 --> 05:20.080
a camera that measures the amount of light that arrives at a photoreceptive surface within

05:20.080 --> 05:21.080
the camera.

05:21.080 --> 05:27.880
While an active sensor is a sensor that emits energy into the environment and measures the

05:27.880 --> 05:28.880
reactions.

05:28.880 --> 05:34.800
And then from that measurement, the sensor tries to deduce some information about the

05:34.800 --> 05:35.800
environment.

05:35.800 --> 05:43.120
Typical active sensors are laser rangefinders that shoot a laser and then measure the signal

05:43.120 --> 05:51.400
that is received back or ultrasonic sensors, whereby what you shoot out is an ultrasound

05:51.400 --> 05:53.400
signal.

05:53.400 --> 06:02.240
Now, one of the advantages of the passive sensors is that they are broadly relatively

06:02.240 --> 06:07.080
cheap and very widely available.

06:07.080 --> 06:12.640
But the disadvantage is that they are sensitive to the environmental conditions.

06:12.640 --> 06:16.200
For example, a camera works well if you have good lighting conditions.

06:16.200 --> 06:21.560
But then if you're in a dark situation, then you don't have a signal anymore.

06:21.560 --> 06:28.840
In context, an active sensor is capable of working on a wider range of conditions.

06:28.840 --> 06:32.880
For example, you emit light in order to illuminate the scene.

06:32.880 --> 06:36.160
But generally, they tend to be more expensive.

06:36.160 --> 06:40.920
So as always, there is a trade-off in terms of how you architect the sensing package for

06:40.920 --> 06:45.400
your robot.

06:45.400 --> 06:49.320
In terms of performance, broadly, sensors are ... yes?

06:49.320 --> 06:54.360
In terms of sensor classification, if you had a system that was, say, like a camera

06:54.360 --> 07:01.320
flash, would that be considered an active sensor system if you really had lights on

07:01.320 --> 07:05.880
and then you had the camera viewing the environment with that light you added to it?

07:05.880 --> 07:13.360
Okay, so this is a relatively broad categorization of sensors.

07:13.360 --> 07:15.700
So there are not very strict guidelines.

07:15.700 --> 07:20.800
But from my point of view, the flash of a camera is an example of active sensing, whereby

07:20.800 --> 07:29.520
you are emitting energy into the environment in order to help with the perception process.

07:29.520 --> 07:35.840
So another important way of reasoning about sensors is how do we specify the performance

07:35.840 --> 07:36.840
of those sensors?

07:36.840 --> 07:42.240
And basically, the performance of the sensors can be specified in terms of design specs

07:42.240 --> 07:43.960
and in-situ performance.

07:43.960 --> 07:51.900
Design specs are characteristics of a sensor that can be measured in a laboratory environment.

07:51.900 --> 07:58.560
So basically, in the plants where the sensors are produced.

07:58.560 --> 08:05.200
In-situ specifications are those that can only be measured when you filled the sensor

08:05.200 --> 08:07.520
in a real-world application.

08:07.520 --> 08:09.280
Let's start with the design specs.

08:09.280 --> 08:14.180
The four main ones are the dynamic range, meaning the ratio between the maximum and

08:14.180 --> 08:17.720
minimum input values that your sensor can measure.

08:17.720 --> 08:23.960
Say for a thermometer, the maximum and minimum temperature that you can measure.

08:23.960 --> 08:30.000
The resolution, so the minimum difference between two values, like one degree Celsius

08:30.000 --> 08:32.960
or 0.1 degrees Celsius and so on.

08:32.960 --> 08:39.720
The linearity, whether the output response is linear with respect to the input signal.

08:39.720 --> 08:44.800
Usually for a sensor, you would like to have a linear response because it simplifies the

08:44.800 --> 08:50.280
number of operations from calibration to the interpretation of the signal and so on.

08:50.280 --> 08:51.760
And finally, the bandwidth of frequency.

08:51.760 --> 08:57.920
So how quickly you get measurement updates from the sensors.

08:57.920 --> 09:02.760
In some cases, you can go up to kilohertz, but from other sensors, you have a much lower

09:02.760 --> 09:03.760
updates.

09:03.760 --> 09:10.000
For example, from leather, usually you have updates in the order of a few hertz.

09:10.000 --> 09:14.540
Another interesting set of specifications are, as I mentioned before, the in-situ specifications.

09:14.540 --> 09:19.360
So those that needed to be characterized in the target environment.

09:19.360 --> 09:25.040
Basically, if you have a robot, you filled it on something that emulates the surface

09:25.040 --> 09:30.560
of Mars or on a cave, and then you really see how your sensor performs.

09:30.560 --> 09:33.760
In particular, they're all very important.

09:33.760 --> 09:37.120
So the first one is the so-called sensitivity.

09:37.120 --> 09:40.280
So the ratio of output change to input change.

09:40.280 --> 09:47.200
So if you change the input, say the amount of light that is coming at your camera, what

09:47.200 --> 09:51.840
is the change in the signal that is coming out of your camera?

09:51.840 --> 09:58.120
Ideally, you would like to have a high sensitivity, but in some cases, that is not the case.

09:58.120 --> 10:05.720
And even more important specification is cross-sensitivity, basically how the output of your sensor is

10:05.720 --> 10:11.080
related to quantities that are not related to your target quantity.

10:11.080 --> 10:17.200
Like, for example, assume that you have a compass to measure your heading.

10:17.200 --> 10:23.280
And so what you want to measure with a compass is basically the deviation between your heading

10:23.280 --> 10:25.040
and the magnetic field of Earth.

10:25.760 --> 10:30.720
But now let's assume that I put some magnets around the compass, then the output of the

10:30.720 --> 10:35.280
compass is going to be perturbed by the fact that you have magnets around you, which means

10:35.280 --> 10:41.280
that your sensor has a high cross-sensitivity with respect to quantities that are not really

10:41.280 --> 10:46.040
related to what you want to really measure.

10:46.040 --> 10:50.720
Of course, in general, you would like to have a cross-sensitivity that is zero, but all

10:50.800 --> 10:55.760
sensors somehow are coupled with the quantities that are not directly related to those that

10:55.760 --> 10:56.920
you want to measure.

10:56.920 --> 10:58.640
So you have to be careful about it.

10:58.640 --> 11:04.040
For example, a compass might be difficult to work with on an autonomous robot where

11:04.040 --> 11:10.560
you have a number of metallic parts because that sensor might exhibit high cross-sensitivity.

11:10.560 --> 11:13.520
The third in-situ specification is the error.

11:13.520 --> 11:18.840
This is probably the most obvious one, which is the difference between what you measure

11:18.880 --> 11:22.880
and the true value of the underlying quantity that you're trying to measure.

11:26.280 --> 11:31.560
And finally, two other specifications that oftentimes are conflated are the notion of

11:31.560 --> 11:33.960
accuracy and the notion of precision.

11:33.960 --> 11:43.280
Accuracy refers to the degree of conformity between what you measure and the true value

11:43.280 --> 11:44.680
in percentages.

11:44.680 --> 11:51.640
So it's defined as 1 minus the magnitude of the error over the true value that you're

11:51.640 --> 11:53.160
trying to measure.

11:53.160 --> 11:59.800
Of course, if the error is zero, it means that your accuracy is 100%.

11:59.800 --> 12:06.840
Precision instead quantifies the degree of reproducibility of the results.

12:06.840 --> 12:19.120
So to be clear, say that x represents the target quantity that you want to measure,

12:19.120 --> 12:22.200
y is the output.

12:22.200 --> 12:32.420
So say that this is the true value v and this is what you measure.

12:32.420 --> 12:39.940
So this difference is related to accuracy, in particular, 1 minus this distance over

12:39.940 --> 12:46.700
the value is the accuracy, while if you carry out multiple measurements and you get a spread

12:46.700 --> 12:53.100
of measurements, this spread is related to precision.

12:53.100 --> 12:57.660
So you might have a sensor that is highly inaccurate, but very precise, which means

12:57.660 --> 13:00.860
that every time it gives you the wrong information.

13:01.300 --> 13:07.900
Likewise, you can have a sensor that is accurate, but imprecise, which means that basically

13:07.900 --> 13:15.060
this sort of Gaussian is centered around the true value, but it's pretty fat.

13:15.060 --> 13:19.500
So the key point here is that precision and accuracy are two distinct concepts, and in

13:19.500 --> 13:23.260
general, you want to have a sensor that is both precise and accurate.

13:24.260 --> 13:28.860
The other sensor that was just precise, but not accurate, isn't that like not the worst

13:28.860 --> 13:31.260
thing in the world, because you get to figure out what the error was and you can practice it?

13:31.260 --> 13:35.260
Exactly, because it's not a very good point.

13:35.260 --> 13:40.780
So if you have something that is very precise, so it's very picked around a given value that

13:40.780 --> 13:45.300
does not reflect the true value, then what you can do, and that's actually what is done

13:45.300 --> 13:52.140
oftentimes in practice, is to estimate the bias, since the bias is very reproducible,

13:52.140 --> 13:57.060
and then subtract it out from the value that you get, and now you have a calibrated sensor

13:57.060 --> 13:59.060
which is also very accurate.

13:59.060 --> 14:00.060
Yes, absolutely.

14:00.060 --> 14:02.660
And that is oftentimes done in practice.

14:05.660 --> 14:08.980
So your question brings us to the topic of errors.

14:08.980 --> 14:14.020
So no matter how accurate and precise your sensor is, you're going to have errors.

14:14.020 --> 14:19.020
In particular, errors are divided into two main categories.

14:19.900 --> 14:24.820
So those, as you were referring to, that are deterministic, and in principle they can be

14:24.820 --> 14:29.340
modeled, maybe with some sophisticated models, but actually they can be modeled, and they

14:29.340 --> 14:34.380
can be corrected for, through a procedure referred to as calibration.

14:34.380 --> 14:39.020
And we will talk in particular about the camera calibration on Thursday.

14:39.020 --> 14:43.820
So in a way, these are the benign errors, stuff that you can correct for.

14:43.820 --> 14:48.820
The other class of errors, those are the more annoying ones, are the random errors.

14:48.820 --> 14:54.500
Those that cannot be predicted with even sophisticated models, so they appear as random

14:54.500 --> 15:01.140
errors, for which maybe you can provide a distribution, a probabilistic distribution

15:01.140 --> 15:04.020
to characterize them, but not much more than that.

15:04.020 --> 15:10.020
And this is typically the case, for example, of outliers in range-finding sensors such

15:10.020 --> 15:11.020
as sliders.

15:11.020 --> 15:16.540
Sometimes you get a point whose distance is completely mischaracterized.

15:17.540 --> 15:23.780
And that has to do with some maybe reflection in your scene that are very difficult to model,

15:23.780 --> 15:27.140
so you just treat that as a random error.

15:27.140 --> 15:34.180
Now, much more could be said on how we characterize these errors, and we will come back to this

15:34.180 --> 15:38.020
topic later on when we talk about localization.

15:38.020 --> 15:46.380
But an important task for the roboticist is to characterize the errors as other systematic

15:46.380 --> 15:54.380
and random, and specifically for those errors that are random, understand how this randomness

15:54.380 --> 15:57.900
propagates through your autonomy stack.

15:57.900 --> 16:02.340
In general, you are putting together sensor measurements from several sensors, and they

16:02.340 --> 16:06.780
are filtered, and then they are used for downstream applications.

16:06.780 --> 16:12.220
So basically, these errors compound along the perception pipeline.

16:12.220 --> 16:15.980
So the question is how this compounding happens.

16:15.980 --> 16:23.260
And if you start with some raw sensors that have some random errors, what do you get as

16:23.260 --> 16:26.740
an output after you start compounding all these errors?

16:26.740 --> 16:33.620
The main result in this area is referred to as the error propagation law.

16:33.620 --> 16:40.020
We will come back to this later on, but for now, I will leave it at that.

16:40.020 --> 16:46.060
So having said that, let's look at some representative sensors, such as encoders, heading sensors,

16:46.060 --> 16:51.660
accelerometers, and IMU, beacons, active ranging, and finally cameras, which is really the sensor

16:51.660 --> 16:56.420
we're going to focus on for the next two or three lectures.

16:56.420 --> 17:01.300
So first of all, an encoder is one of the most basic sensors that you can have on an

17:01.300 --> 17:04.580
autonomous robot, in particular, a wheeled autonomous robot.

17:04.580 --> 17:13.620
An encoder is, in a nutshell, a device that converts motion into a sequence of digital pulses.

17:13.620 --> 17:22.380
The way it works is that you have a light that shines through a disk that has some slits

17:22.380 --> 17:24.300
on the outer ring.

17:24.300 --> 17:30.940
As this disk moves, think of this disk being rigidly attached to the wheel, then the light

17:30.940 --> 17:34.420
either goes through or it's blocked.

17:34.420 --> 17:41.860
And on the other end of the disk, you have a photoreceptive surface that receives a sequence

17:41.860 --> 17:48.520
of pulses, the frequency of which is related to how quickly this disk is rotating.

17:48.520 --> 17:54.500
So you can now think how, basically, you can translate the frequency at which you measure

17:54.500 --> 17:59.140
these pulses to the speed of the wheel.

17:59.140 --> 18:05.020
And once you know how quickly your wheels are spinning, then by integration, you can

18:05.020 --> 18:12.420
also infer how long you have traveled, which refers to the process of wheel odometry.

18:12.420 --> 18:15.260
Now, in many situations, this is accurate.

18:15.260 --> 18:17.580
In many other situations, this is very inaccurate.

18:17.580 --> 18:21.780
So can you imagine a situation where wheel odometry can be very inaccurate?

18:21.780 --> 18:22.780
Yeah.

18:22.780 --> 18:32.060
Lots of rapidly changing speeds, maybe, so you have to keep changing your integration

18:32.060 --> 18:33.060
and...

18:33.060 --> 18:36.660
In principle, if that is done correctly, it's not a problem.

18:36.660 --> 18:37.660
Yeah.

18:37.660 --> 18:38.660
Wheel slippage.

18:38.660 --> 18:39.660
Exactly.

18:39.660 --> 18:47.300
So, for example, notoriously, wheel odometry is not really useful, for example, in Mars

18:47.300 --> 18:52.020
rovers, because on Mars you have a lot of sand, so you may have wheels that spin in

18:52.020 --> 18:56.020
place, which means that your encoder correctly is measuring that your wheel is spinning

18:56.020 --> 19:01.140
and gives you the right angular speed at which it's spinning, but that is not related to

19:01.140 --> 19:02.460
the distance you are traveling.

19:02.460 --> 19:04.260
Maybe you're just spinning in place.

19:04.260 --> 19:08.540
That's why there is another technique that we're going to cover later on, which is referred

19:08.540 --> 19:15.060
to as a visual odometry, whereby you try to estimate your ego motion by looking at camera

19:15.060 --> 19:18.540
images, which is, by the way, what is done on Mars rovers.

19:18.540 --> 19:19.540
Yeah.

19:19.540 --> 19:32.220
So there are additional slits, for example, here, and additional devices that also allow

19:32.220 --> 19:38.340
you to infer the direction, but here, for this lecture, I just want to give you the

19:38.340 --> 19:39.340
high-level idea.

19:39.340 --> 19:43.860
I don't want to go into the details of each sensor.

19:43.860 --> 19:49.300
There is a few things more to make encoders work, but in a nutshell, this is a basic idea.

19:49.540 --> 19:51.540
But that's a good point.

19:53.540 --> 19:54.540
All right.

19:54.540 --> 19:57.380
Second class of sensors referred to as heading sensor.

19:57.380 --> 20:03.380
Basically, what is the orientation of your product with respect to some reference coordinate

20:03.380 --> 20:04.380
system?

20:04.380 --> 20:09.220
Heading sensors can be both proprioceptive or acceleroceptive.

20:09.220 --> 20:16.500
A proprioceptive sensor, a very important example of a proprioceptive heading sensor

20:16.500 --> 20:19.140
is the so-called gyroscope.

20:19.140 --> 20:29.620
The way that a gyroscope works is that the angular momentum associated with a spinning

20:29.620 --> 20:34.020
wheel keeps the axis of rotation of that wheel inertially stable.

20:34.020 --> 20:37.900
So if you know about angular momentum, this is a well-known fact.

20:37.900 --> 20:39.180
If you don't, no worries.

20:39.180 --> 20:40.980
This is just to give you a high-level idea.

20:40.980 --> 20:46.820
But the idea is that, consider this disk, assume that this disk has a substantial inertia,

20:46.820 --> 20:55.020
and as this disk is rotating, then the angular momentum keeps the axis of rotation inertially

20:55.020 --> 20:56.020
stable.

20:56.020 --> 21:02.420
What that means is that if you mount the spinning wheel within a gimbal system, no torque can

21:02.420 --> 21:06.940
be transmitted from the outer gimbal to the wheel, which basically means that this axis

21:06.940 --> 21:10.420
stays inertially stable so it doesn't move.

21:10.420 --> 21:17.980
So that allows you to infer what is your heading with respect to a fixed reference

21:17.980 --> 21:20.020
system.

21:20.020 --> 21:23.980
The other example of a heading sensor that is acceleroceptive is the same sensor that

21:23.980 --> 21:28.100
I mentioned before, which is the compass.

21:28.100 --> 21:35.020
Now, gyroscopes are widely used, and as we will see later on, they're used particularly

21:35.020 --> 21:40.220
in the context of an IMU, National Measurement Unit.

21:40.220 --> 21:43.900
There are more advanced versions of gyroscopes, for example optical gyroscopes, that do not

21:43.900 --> 21:50.100
rely on mechanical devices, but they're much more expensive.

21:50.100 --> 21:57.700
One of the issues with gyroscopes is that there must still be some friction on the pivots

21:57.700 --> 22:05.620
of the gimbals, which basically creates a drift, basically slowly inaccurate measurements

22:05.620 --> 22:07.220
in your heading.

22:07.220 --> 22:13.500
Now, this is a problem because if you rely only on a gyroscope in order to infer the

22:13.500 --> 22:19.060
trajectory that you have executed, since the drift, meaning the error that you have at

22:19.060 --> 22:24.140
each instant in time, accumulates, after a while, you might end up in a situation whereby

22:24.140 --> 22:29.380
you think you're in a position where you have a heading, but your country has a completely

22:29.380 --> 22:30.600
different heading.

22:30.600 --> 22:36.260
Which basically means that from time to time, you have to periodically get fixes with other

22:36.300 --> 22:45.300
sensors in order to fix the drift in your proprioception sensor.

22:45.300 --> 22:48.180
All right.

22:48.180 --> 22:51.620
So we talked about the heading sensors.

22:51.620 --> 22:54.820
Another important class of sensors is referred to as accelerometers.

22:54.820 --> 23:01.540
So these are sensors that measure forces, say force of gravity is the most basic one,

23:01.540 --> 23:03.360
but all sorts of forces.

23:03.360 --> 23:06.480
The way they work is remarkably simple.

23:06.480 --> 23:14.960
So the whole idea is to have a spring damper system with a proof mass m.

23:14.960 --> 23:18.560
So m is a proof mass inside your sensor.

23:18.560 --> 23:23.800
And then you have a damper, and then you have a spring.

23:23.800 --> 23:31.080
So the question of motion of this proof mass is simply given by a second order differential

23:31.080 --> 23:42.640
equation, whereby the acceleration of the proof mass is equal to the force that you

23:42.640 --> 23:44.560
apply to the system.

23:44.560 --> 23:49.480
And then you have to account also for the damping force due to the damper and to the

23:49.480 --> 23:51.560
elastic force due to the spring.

23:51.560 --> 23:57.080
So that's a prototypical spring mass system with an external force.

23:58.080 --> 24:04.840
The interesting aspect of this device is that assume that the applied force, say gravity,

24:04.840 --> 24:06.520
is a constant.

24:06.520 --> 24:12.040
So what this will cause is that initially this mass will start to oscillate.

24:12.040 --> 24:19.960
But since you have a constant driving force, this mass, you have a damper, a damping term.

24:19.960 --> 24:26.100
Then this oscillation will dampen out, and it will reach a steady state condition.

24:26.140 --> 24:31.020
The steady state condition is the condition whereby x, meaning the displacement between

24:31.020 --> 24:36.140
where the mass is and the equilibrium position for the mass.

24:36.140 --> 24:38.740
The steady state is when that x is a constant.

24:38.740 --> 24:42.900
So if you plug x equal to constant here in this differential equation, you see that the

24:42.900 --> 24:49.260
steady state value for x has to satisfy the kx over m equal to the acceleration applied.

24:49.260 --> 24:54.300
Which basically means that if you can measure the steady state displacement of the proof

24:54.340 --> 24:59.820
mass from the equilibrium position, and if you know the mass or the proof mass and the

24:59.820 --> 25:06.500
elastic coefficient for your spring, then you can measure the acceleration that has

25:06.500 --> 25:07.500
been applied.

25:07.500 --> 25:11.020
So, for example, if it is gravity, then you can measure gravity.

25:11.020 --> 25:15.140
Obviously, you know gravity, so you wouldn't use this in general to measure gravity, but

25:15.140 --> 25:19.660
you would use it to measure other forces that are impacting your robots.

25:19.660 --> 25:22.420
Does it make sense?

25:22.420 --> 25:28.980
So the idea is fairly simple, to just use a proof mass whose motion is influenced by

25:28.980 --> 25:36.980
external forces with the idea that if the external force is constant, almost specifically

25:36.980 --> 25:42.220
is slowly time varying with respect to the time constants of the spring mass system,

25:42.220 --> 25:45.160
then you can measure the force that is applied.

25:45.160 --> 25:52.880
In practice, nowadays, this is implemented by using MEMS, micro-mechanical systems, whereby

25:52.880 --> 25:58.840
you have a cantilever with a very small mass, and the deflection of this cantilever beam

25:58.840 --> 26:02.760
is measured with other piezoelectric capacity sensors.

26:02.760 --> 26:11.920
So this can be made extremely compact.

26:11.920 --> 26:12.920
So MEMS are great.

26:12.920 --> 26:16.080
The only problem is that they tend to be fragile.

26:16.080 --> 26:22.040
And one of the issues with that is that, for example, if you have a child, and she likes

26:22.040 --> 26:28.060
to play with your phone, then she likes to drop your phone on the ground.

26:28.060 --> 26:34.720
And then after four or five times, the accelerometer slash gyroscope system is gone.

26:34.720 --> 26:39.320
For example, when my phone, when you turn it, it doesn't turn anymore.

26:39.320 --> 26:43.360
And the phone of my wife, when it turns, the image doesn't turn anymore.

26:43.360 --> 26:47.640
That's because, basically, these MEMS sensors are nice, but if you abuse them, they are

26:47.640 --> 26:50.720
the first ones to fail.

26:50.720 --> 26:53.820
So treat your phone with care.

26:53.820 --> 26:55.880
So we know how to measure headings.

26:55.880 --> 26:58.800
We know how to measure acceleration.

26:58.800 --> 27:05.680
Then we can put everything together, and we then are able to measure both our translational

27:05.680 --> 27:07.800
motion and our rotational motion.

27:07.800 --> 27:14.340
In particular, the sensor, whereby you combine both accelerometric measurements and the gyroscopic

27:14.340 --> 27:18.840
measurements, is referred to as IMU, Inertial Measurement Unit.

27:18.840 --> 27:25.040
So this is a sensor that you will hear a lot as you work with autonomous robots.

27:25.040 --> 27:30.800
So the idea is to use a gyroscope that allows you to infer angular velocity, and then through

27:30.800 --> 27:33.720
integration the orientation of your robot.

27:33.720 --> 27:35.860
Then you have an accelerometer.

27:35.860 --> 27:42.260
And then through repeated integration of your acceleration, then you get the velocity estimate,

27:42.260 --> 27:46.380
and then you get an estimate of your position.

27:46.380 --> 27:49.760
You know that velocity is the integral of acceleration.

27:49.760 --> 27:54.920
Position is the integral of velocity.

27:54.920 --> 28:00.660
So this is great, because assume that I give you initial condition about your position,

28:00.660 --> 28:10.940
about your velocity, then by using IMU, basically measuring the accelerations to which your

28:10.940 --> 28:19.380
robot is, the station that your robot is undergoing, and the angular velocities that your robot

28:19.380 --> 28:23.220
is undergoing, I can estimate where I am.

28:23.260 --> 28:30.980
And for example, IMUs are widely used in the context of aircraft navigation, so that at

28:30.980 --> 28:36.180
any point in time, you know more or less where you are.

28:36.180 --> 28:44.100
IMUs were really spearheaded after the Second World War.

28:44.100 --> 28:54.340
But again, this procedure of only relying on proprioceptive information is referred

28:54.340 --> 29:01.540
to as dead reckoning, where you have to be cautious because errors compound, and at some

29:01.540 --> 29:05.620
point you end up in a situation that is very different from where you are.

29:05.660 --> 29:14.180
For example, in the 80s, there was an airplane from Korean Airlines.

29:14.180 --> 29:23.240
So basically this is Russia, this is Kamchatka Peninsula, so this is the Soviet Union.

29:23.240 --> 29:26.860
So here there is Korea, more or less.

29:26.860 --> 29:32.740
Here there is Alaska, United States, Alaska.

29:32.740 --> 29:39.420
This was a plane going from New York to Seoul, and then stopped over in Alaska, at the time

29:39.420 --> 29:45.860
there was not enough capacity to go in one shot from New York to Seoul, and then going

29:45.860 --> 29:47.040
to Korea.

29:47.040 --> 29:54.500
So the flight plan was to stay out of the airspace of the Soviet Union, but due to an

29:54.500 --> 30:01.380
issue with the Inertial Measurement Unit, right after taking off from Anchorage, the

30:01.380 --> 30:08.380
airplane started drifting, and it was night, so the pilots didn't have any other external

30:08.380 --> 30:11.300
reference in order to understand that the plane was drifting.

30:11.300 --> 30:17.660
So finally they crossed a peninsula, and then the Soviet planes were scrambled, then they

30:17.660 --> 30:21.460
got out of the peninsula, so they were again in international waters, but then they crossed

30:21.460 --> 30:27.980
another peninsula, and here they were intercepted by a military airplane from the Soviet Union

30:27.980 --> 30:29.660
and shot down.

30:29.660 --> 30:37.460
So unfortunately, this is an example of accidents that happen because of, well, sort of accident,

30:37.460 --> 30:41.220
that happen because of a drift in an Inertial Measurement Unit.

30:41.220 --> 30:45.660
But having said that, Inertial Measurement Units can be remarkably accurate.

30:45.660 --> 30:52.420
But the key point is that they often need to be augmented with some procedure to fix

30:52.420 --> 30:58.180
the drift from time to time, GPS, other beacons, and so on.

30:58.180 --> 31:03.380
In particular, beacons are signal devices with precisely known positions.

31:03.380 --> 31:08.180
So if you can measure the distance between your robot and those beacons, then you know

31:08.180 --> 31:15.500
exactly where your robot is, assuming that you have a precise knowledge about where those

31:15.500 --> 31:17.540
sensors are.

31:17.540 --> 31:25.640
One of the most typical beacons is a lighthouse, but of course the most important beacon system

31:25.640 --> 31:31.880
out there is represented by GPS, a constellation of satellites that allow you, assuming that

31:31.880 --> 31:39.240
you have a GPS receiver, to pinpoint your position almost anywhere on Earth.

31:39.240 --> 31:44.400
The last class of sensors that I want to discuss is the class of active ranging sensors.

31:44.400 --> 31:49.960
So those sensors that emit energy into the environment in order to gather information

31:49.960 --> 31:55.080
about the environment, for example, in terms of distances to objects.

31:55.080 --> 31:58.640
There are two main types of ranging sensors.

31:58.640 --> 32:02.520
The first one is called Time-of-Flight Active Ranging Sensors.

32:02.520 --> 32:12.600
So these are sensors that emit electromagnetic waves, or sound waves, and they measure how

32:12.600 --> 32:18.360
long it takes for that wave to come back to the sensor itself.

32:18.360 --> 32:23.160
The second class of active ranging sensors are the sensors that use geometric properties

32:23.160 --> 32:27.120
in the measurements in order to reconstruct the environment.

32:27.120 --> 32:34.280
For example, emitting some pattern in the environment, see how this pattern is deformed,

32:34.280 --> 32:37.680
and then from that infer properties about the environment.

32:37.680 --> 32:41.240
So let's discuss each sensor in turn.

32:41.240 --> 32:46.600
The idea, as I said, of a time-of-flight ranging sensor is to make use of the propagation of

32:46.600 --> 32:50.960
the speed of sound or of an electromagnetic wave and measure how long it takes for that

32:50.960 --> 32:58.240
wave to come back to the sensor once it is reflected by an object.

32:58.240 --> 33:04.160
So assuming that you know the speed of propagation of that wave, let's call that speed c, then

33:04.160 --> 33:10.800
the time it takes for that wave to travel to do the round trip from the sensor to the

33:10.800 --> 33:18.200
object and back is equal to d, the distance over c, or the distance traveled is equal

33:18.200 --> 33:31.800
to the speed of the wave times the time it takes for the signal to go back and forth.

33:31.800 --> 33:36.840
So here's your sensor, this is an object.

33:36.840 --> 33:43.360
So you emit an electromagnetic wave, it comes back, so the round trip distance is equal

33:43.360 --> 33:48.160
to d, and this round trip distance is equal to c times t.

33:48.160 --> 33:55.280
c is the speed, t is the time it takes from the time it was emitted to the time its reflection

33:55.280 --> 33:56.960
is measured back.

33:56.960 --> 34:05.080
So then once, assuming that you are able to measure the round trip time t, how do you

34:05.080 --> 34:10.000
estimate the actual distance from an obstacle?

34:10.000 --> 34:15.360
So what is this, the obstacle, this distance here?

34:15.360 --> 34:29.080
Yeah, this was not supposed to be a particularly difficult question, that's it.

34:29.080 --> 34:35.000
Of course, with the electromagnetic waves they go fast, so these times are in general

34:35.000 --> 34:36.080
extremely short.

34:36.080 --> 34:41.920
So the circuitry needed in order to make this idea work is rather complicated, but by now

34:41.920 --> 34:44.400
this technology is widely available.

34:44.400 --> 34:53.100
Prosely difficult sensors in this class is represented by ladders, laser rangefinders.

34:53.100 --> 34:59.720
Now the performance of the sensor depends on a number of properties of the sensor itself

34:59.720 --> 35:00.720
and of the environment.

35:00.720 --> 35:05.080
In particular, you need to be able to determine the exact time of arrival, which is not easy

35:05.080 --> 35:06.080
in general.

35:06.080 --> 35:11.720
That's why ladders are still relatively expensive, for example, the one that you use on your

35:11.720 --> 35:18.240
robot is about $8,000, so do not bring it on.

35:18.240 --> 35:25.640
And the sensor also, the performance of the sensor also depends on the interaction with

35:25.640 --> 35:30.800
the target, meaning if you have several different reflections and so on.

35:31.800 --> 35:35.880
The second class of active-ranging sensors is represented by geometric active-ranging

35:35.880 --> 35:38.360
sensors, which are fairly interesting sensors.

35:38.360 --> 35:44.280
The idea is, as I said, to exploit geometric properties of the readings in order to infer

35:44.280 --> 35:45.280
structure.

35:45.280 --> 35:54.120
Like, for example, a particular important class of sensor in this class is represented

35:54.120 --> 36:00.440
by sensors that emit structural light, so basically light with a pattern.

36:00.440 --> 36:06.140
Say like dots, maybe you may not see them because maybe they are in the infrared wavelength,

36:06.140 --> 36:07.940
but they emit a pattern.

36:07.940 --> 36:13.680
And then you measure how you observe how this pattern is deformed, and from that you can

36:13.680 --> 36:18.660
infer the shape of the objects that are in the environment.

36:18.660 --> 36:23.480
So this is, for example, the technique that is used by the Xbox and also by the Intel

36:23.480 --> 36:24.480
RealSense sensor.

36:30.480 --> 36:34.480
Are one of these two classes of sensors more or less expensive on average than geometric

36:34.480 --> 36:37.360
versus the time of flight?

36:37.360 --> 36:41.320
So these sensors tend to be, they're still more expensive than cameras, but in general

36:41.320 --> 36:43.800
much less expensive than time of flight.

36:43.800 --> 36:52.040
Time of flight has this very complicated circuitry in order to measure time, and also they try

36:52.040 --> 36:56.480
to address two different markets.

36:56.520 --> 37:02.280
So these sensors is more to reconstruct structure close by, say three to four meters, while

37:02.280 --> 37:08.080
with ladders you want to have a dense point cloud representing your environment several

37:08.080 --> 37:14.800
meters away, which means that you have to pump more energy in order to get some signal

37:14.800 --> 37:15.800
back.

37:15.800 --> 37:24.360
Indeed, one of the limitations about pumping energy is that you don't want to have this

37:24.360 --> 37:27.000
sensor being harmful for the human eye.

37:27.000 --> 37:28.960
So that's actually kind of the upper limit.

37:28.960 --> 37:33.800
In principle, you could have ladders that allow you to see farther than what is currently

37:33.800 --> 37:37.360
possible, but that would imply that you have to pump more energy, and that will make them

37:37.360 --> 37:43.040
dangerous for the human eye, because of the frequency at which these ladders operate.

37:43.040 --> 37:49.520
So one of the current research directions in this topic, and what several start-ups

37:49.520 --> 37:54.800
right now in the Bay Area are working on, is to try to use ladders at different frequencies

37:54.800 --> 38:01.200
that do not interfere with the operation of the human eye, so that you can pump more energy.

38:01.200 --> 38:06.480
Unfortunately, those different frequencies are difficult to generate with lasers that

38:06.480 --> 38:08.520
are embedded in silicon.

38:08.520 --> 38:13.680
So that's the engineering challenge, to be able to embed lasers on silicon wafers at

38:13.680 --> 38:17.600
the frequencies that are not harmful for the human eye.

38:18.200 --> 38:20.840
And there are a number of companies out there that try to do that.

38:20.840 --> 38:27.360
So these are cheaper than time-of-flight range sensors, and this sensor also addresses two

38:27.360 --> 38:28.360
different markets.

38:28.360 --> 38:33.040
This is more for short-range sensing, the other one is for more long-range sensing.

38:33.040 --> 38:38.360
For very long-range sensing, you would use a radar.

38:38.360 --> 38:44.800
And radar, it's interesting that radars are currently being designed specifically for

38:44.800 --> 38:52.720
the purposes of robot autonomy, before seeing objects at very long distances, like airplanes

38:52.720 --> 38:57.480
and so on, but not so much for observing objects in structural environments like cities.

38:57.480 --> 39:04.520
Now, there are many other sensors, like radars, as I just mentioned, tactile sensors, and

39:04.520 --> 39:09.280
there are many other sensors that are currently emerging, like for example, artificial skins,

39:09.280 --> 39:13.520
basically skins that you can put on a robot, and allows the robot to measure pressure,

39:13.520 --> 39:21.280
to measure temperature and so on, or also neuromorphic cameras, so cameras that work

39:21.280 --> 39:29.680
in a way that is more similar to how the human eye works, as opposed to what your current

39:29.680 --> 39:31.120
camera works.

39:31.120 --> 39:36.560
Anyway, so this discussion was meant to only provide a general overview of what are the

39:36.560 --> 39:41.320
sensors out there, what are the benefits and disadvantages of each one.

39:41.320 --> 39:47.920
And now, we're going to focus on a very important sensor for robots, which is the camera.

39:47.920 --> 39:50.400
Any questions so far?

39:50.400 --> 39:57.200
You should be glad that for 40 minutes, you didn't have differential equations on the

39:57.200 --> 39:58.200
board.

39:58.200 --> 40:00.400
Now, we go to mathematical formulas.

40:00.400 --> 40:06.800
Cool, so cameras, objective for today is to give an introduction to computer vision, and

40:06.800 --> 40:11.000
in particular to cameras and cameras models.

40:11.000 --> 40:16.000
So my discussion about cameras is taken by three main references.

40:16.000 --> 40:22.280
One is a book from Sigur Nurbakas and Scaramuzza, and the two additional books that are considered

40:22.280 --> 40:30.560
references for computer vision, The Forcities and Ponds, and The Hartley and Zisserman.

40:30.560 --> 40:35.880
These books go into much more details than what is needed for this class, but I just

40:35.880 --> 40:40.400
provide the references for you, in case you want to get more detail about the material

40:40.400 --> 40:43.240
that I'm going to explain.

40:43.240 --> 40:50.480
In particular, a camera is basically a sensor that tries to replicate the sense of vision,

40:50.480 --> 40:56.720
whereby through our eyes, we're able to interpret the surrounding environment using light in

40:56.720 --> 41:01.040
the visible spectrum that is reflected by objects in the environment.

41:01.040 --> 41:06.340
Through our eyes, amazingly, we can capture an enormous amount of information.

41:06.340 --> 41:10.840
So I just try to do so in a similar way.

41:10.840 --> 41:15.540
In particular, of course, the inner working of a camera is very different from the inner

41:15.540 --> 41:17.660
working of a human eye.

41:17.660 --> 41:22.640
In particular, the way that the camera works is that you have a lens through which you

41:22.640 --> 41:28.260
capture the ambient light, and this ambient light is converted into a digital image that

41:28.260 --> 41:31.920
is then processed in order to extract the relevant information.

41:31.920 --> 41:39.660
So today, and on Thursday, we are going to talk about how a camera goes from capturing

41:39.660 --> 41:46.280
light into converting that light into an image that we can extract information from.

41:46.280 --> 41:51.520
Next week, we'll talk about how to extract information from a camera image.

41:51.520 --> 41:53.960
That's the plan.

41:53.960 --> 42:00.880
So let's try to get a better understanding of how an image of the world is captured through

42:00.880 --> 42:03.120
a camera.

42:03.120 --> 42:04.520
So the idea is as follows.

42:04.520 --> 42:13.040
So here there is the sun, the light is reflected by an object, say a tree, and this is scattered

42:13.040 --> 42:14.900
in many different directions.

42:14.900 --> 42:19.360
So let's assume that we place a photoreceptive surface somewhere.

42:19.360 --> 42:25.600
A photoreceptive surface is a surface that is capable of capturing light and translating

42:25.600 --> 42:32.200
that light into a digital signal that can be then stored in your camera.

42:32.200 --> 42:39.840
Now the problem with this approach, so basically just placing a photoreceptive surface, is

42:39.840 --> 42:47.760
that a point on the tree is going to scatter light in many different directions, which

42:47.760 --> 42:56.900
means that a point on the photoreceptive surface is going to be impinged by light rays that

42:56.900 --> 43:04.020
come from several different points on the object that you're trying to image.

43:04.020 --> 43:11.480
Which means that it's not possible to correlate one to one the light that you get at a given

43:11.480 --> 43:16.360
position of your photoreceptive surface with a point on the image.

43:16.360 --> 43:21.020
Basically this means that your image is extremely blurred.

43:21.020 --> 43:27.280
It's basically the averaging of rays of light coming from several different points.

43:27.280 --> 43:31.120
What you get is basically something that is not intelligible.

43:31.120 --> 43:38.320
So the whole idea of a camera is to, in a nutshell, establish a one-to-one mapping between

43:38.320 --> 43:43.520
points on an object and points on your photoreceptive surface.

43:43.520 --> 43:49.760
And the idea to do so is to place in between your photoreceptive surface an environment

43:49.760 --> 43:59.080
that you're trying to visualize a screen with a very small aperture.

43:59.080 --> 44:07.480
The idea is that this screen is going to block almost all of the light, except for those

44:07.480 --> 44:14.160
rays, light rays, that go through the aperture.

44:14.160 --> 44:23.860
So in the limits that this hole is very, very small, only one light ray from each point

44:23.860 --> 44:26.520
is going to go through this hole.

44:26.520 --> 44:33.160
Which means that on the photoreceptive surface, for each point on that surface, you only have

44:33.160 --> 44:35.960
a corresponding point on the object.

44:35.960 --> 44:40.400
So basically you're now able to establish a one-to-one mapping between points on an

44:40.400 --> 44:47.920
object and points on a photoreceptive surface that, from a physical standpoint, allows your

44:47.920 --> 44:55.640
image to be very crisp and a faithful representation of your environment.

44:55.640 --> 44:59.640
Does this make sense?

45:00.640 --> 45:06.160
This is the simplest possible way you can explain how vision works.

45:06.160 --> 45:10.240
This hole is usually referred to as a pinhole.

45:10.240 --> 45:17.740
And that's literally how, at the most foundational level, a camera works.

45:17.740 --> 45:26.120
So now the engineering challenge is to be able to translate these concepts into a device.

45:26.120 --> 45:36.000
As many good ideas in engineering, this idea came from an Italian, Leonardo da Vinci, 1502.

45:36.000 --> 45:44.600
So he was the first one to provide a very clear description about how a camera could

45:44.600 --> 45:45.800
work.

45:45.800 --> 45:51.860
The first known drawing of the concept of a pinhole camera.

45:51.860 --> 46:01.360
So this idea of placing a surface that reflects most of the light, except for the light that

46:01.360 --> 46:02.800
goes through a pinhole.

46:02.800 --> 46:05.760
And then that light goes into a photoreceptive surface.

46:05.760 --> 46:11.560
So the first image was published in 1544.

46:11.560 --> 46:13.080
And this is basically the idea.

46:13.080 --> 46:17.820
So you have an object, you have a surface that reflects almost all the light, except

46:17.820 --> 46:19.920
the one that goes through the hole.

46:19.920 --> 46:26.360
And then you get, at a photoreceptive surface, the image inverted.

46:26.360 --> 46:30.380
So this device is also referred to as a camera obscura.

46:30.380 --> 46:37.340
If you go to San Francisco, close to the Golden Gate, there is a restaurant called The Cliff.

46:37.340 --> 46:43.300
If you go down the stairs, there is a small camera obscura that you can visit, just in

46:43.300 --> 46:47.220
case you happen to swing by there.

46:47.220 --> 46:54.620
So let's get a little bit more into the technical definition of a pinhole camera.

46:54.620 --> 47:01.840
So a camera whereby you visualize the environment through this notion of a pinhole.

47:01.840 --> 47:06.000
The hole, as I said, is referred to as the pinhole.

47:06.000 --> 47:14.600
The distance between the surface that blocks most of the light and the photoreceptive surface

47:14.700 --> 47:19.460
is referred to as the focal length.

47:19.460 --> 47:25.540
The photoreceptive surface, that is the surface where you record the image, is referred to

47:25.540 --> 47:29.860
as the image plane.

47:29.860 --> 47:41.600
Now the way that this works is that an object in the physical space gets imaged on the image

47:41.620 --> 47:49.620
plane in a way that is a scaled-down version and is inverted.

47:49.620 --> 47:57.700
Oftentimes, to make the analysis simpler, one imagines a virtual image plane, which

47:57.700 --> 48:12.440
is placed a distance equal to the focal length in front of the plane that is blocking the

48:12.440 --> 48:13.440
light.

48:13.440 --> 48:15.800
Now this is a virtual image plane.

48:15.800 --> 48:19.560
The advantage of this virtual image plane is that in this virtual image plane, what

48:19.560 --> 48:25.200
you get is an image that is exactly the same as the one that is actually imaged by your

48:25.200 --> 48:27.560
camera but is not inverted.

48:27.560 --> 48:36.740
So for mathematical conveniences, sometimes we'll be using the notion of a virtual image

48:36.740 --> 48:37.740
plane.

48:37.740 --> 48:46.080
I just want to make sure that the anomaly is confused by it.

48:46.080 --> 48:54.380
The process through which an image is imaged is referred to as a prospective projection.

48:54.380 --> 48:58.120
And this is a very important concept because it allows you to relate an image in the physical

48:58.120 --> 49:04.280
world into an image into the image plane, which means that we have to dig a little bit

49:04.280 --> 49:08.760
deeper in how the prospective projection works in order to go to the next step.

49:08.760 --> 49:15.120
That is really what we care about, meaning how do we extract information from images.

49:15.120 --> 49:20.440
So let's get a little bit deeper on how a prospective projection works.

49:20.500 --> 49:26.580
I know there's a bit of notation, but the underlying idea is relatively simple.

49:26.580 --> 49:31.460
So this is the mathematical abstraction of a pinhole camera.

49:31.460 --> 49:34.420
O is what we refer to as the pinhole.

49:34.420 --> 49:41.420
And we assume that we attach a reference system to the camera that has the origin center of

49:41.420 --> 49:44.020
the pinhole.

49:44.020 --> 49:55.880
The z-axis oriented along the axis that goes through the pinhole and is orthogonal to the

49:55.880 --> 50:00.520
image plane, this axis is referred to as the optical axis.

50:00.520 --> 50:09.040
And the z-axis for the camera reference frame has the same direction of the optical axis,

50:09.040 --> 50:15.040
and the direction is in the direction of the image plane.

50:15.040 --> 50:21.360
And then we have the x-axis that is parallel to the x-axis, the image plane, and then the

50:21.360 --> 50:24.640
y-axis that completes the orthogonal turn.

50:24.640 --> 50:29.560
This is a camera reference system.

50:29.560 --> 50:39.020
The point whereby the optical axis intersects the image plane is referred to as the image

50:39.020 --> 50:41.660
center.

50:41.660 --> 50:50.040
Now the question is, assume that we have a point that has coordinates x, y, z with respect

50:50.040 --> 50:53.720
to the camera reference frame.

50:53.720 --> 50:58.760
How is this point capital P imaged into the image plane?

50:58.760 --> 51:07.900
Meaning what are the coordinates of P, small p, the point that is imaged in the image plane,

51:07.900 --> 51:11.820
with respect to the camera reference frame?

51:11.820 --> 51:16.940
So this transformation is really what is referred to as the perspective transformation.

51:16.940 --> 51:18.860
Is this setup clear?

51:18.860 --> 51:20.800
We have a camera reference frame.

51:20.800 --> 51:23.900
We have a point P that is the point in the physical world.

51:23.900 --> 51:29.780
We want to understand how those coordinates translate into the coordinates for the point

51:29.780 --> 51:36.180
that is the representation in the image plane of that point in the physical space.

51:36.180 --> 51:42.900
Or in other words, if I give you information about the key parameters of the camera, how

51:42.900 --> 51:48.700
do I find a mapping between uppercase XYZ and lowercase XYZ?

51:48.700 --> 51:56.280
Again, lowercase XYZ are the coordinate of lowercase P, uppercase XYZ are the coordinate

51:56.280 --> 52:01.400
of uppercase P, the point in the physical space.

52:01.400 --> 52:06.580
As before, the distance between the pinhole and image plane is referred to as the focal

52:06.580 --> 52:11.580
length.

52:11.580 --> 52:14.000
Is this setup clear?

52:14.000 --> 52:16.240
All right.

52:16.240 --> 52:23.640
So this mapping is indeed relatively simple to derive.

52:23.640 --> 52:32.340
In particular, let's look at the vectors OP and the vectors OP.

52:32.340 --> 52:41.900
So Op is the vector that goes from the origin of the camera reference frame to the point

52:41.900 --> 52:49.160
P, the projected point in the image plane.

52:49.160 --> 52:58.540
The vector O, capital P, is the vector that goes from the pinhole to the point in the

52:58.540 --> 53:01.740
real space, in the real obstacle.

53:01.740 --> 53:09.300
Now as you can see, since these two vectors lie on the same line, these two vectors are

53:09.300 --> 53:11.220
parallel.

53:11.240 --> 53:21.320
So by definition, two vectors V1 and V2 are parallel if they can be written as V1 equal

53:21.320 --> 53:27.940
to lambda V2 for some lambda in the reals.

53:27.940 --> 53:32.600
That's how you represent mathematically the fact that the two vectors are parallel to

53:32.600 --> 53:34.000
each other.

53:34.000 --> 53:39.520
In particular, lambda represents a scaling factor, and its sign represents whether the

53:39.520 --> 53:43.560
vectors are pointing in the same direction or in opposite directions.

53:43.560 --> 53:47.800
In this case, these two vectors are pointing in opposite directions, so the sign will be

53:47.800 --> 53:50.300
negative.

53:50.300 --> 53:58.040
So in order to find the transformation, then what you do is simply to represent OP with

53:58.040 --> 54:07.500
its coordinates x, y, z, O capital P with its coordinates x, y, z, or uppercase.

54:07.500 --> 54:16.660
Since these two vectors are parallel, then OP has to be equal to lambda O capital P for

54:16.660 --> 54:27.820
some lambda, which implies, by taking this equality component-wise, that small x is equal

54:27.820 --> 54:39.300
to lambda big X, y is equal to small lambda big Y, z is equal to small lambda big Z.

54:39.300 --> 54:51.100
Which means that lambda is equal to small x over capital

54:51.100 --> 54:59.740
X, lambda is equal to small y over capital Y, and lambda is equal to small z over capital

54:59.740 --> 55:03.140
Z.

55:03.140 --> 55:16.940
And now we can then write x as lambda big X, but let's plug this in.

55:16.940 --> 55:28.240
So what we get is small x is equal to small z over big Z times capital X.

55:28.240 --> 55:37.100
The last step is to recognize that the vertical coordinates for the point at its image, so

55:37.100 --> 55:43.140
small z, is equal to the focal length, f, by definition.

55:43.140 --> 55:49.580
So this would be f, a parameter that we assume we know, in reality, it has to be calibrated

55:49.580 --> 55:50.580
for.

55:50.580 --> 55:55.580
We discussed on Thursday how to calibrate for the focal length, but for now, let's assume

55:55.580 --> 55:59.940
that the parameters of the camera are known, and in particular, the focal length is known.

55:59.940 --> 56:09.380
So in this way, we have found the mapping, for example, small y is equal to f over big

56:09.380 --> 56:15.020
Z times y.

56:15.020 --> 56:22.420
So these equations allow us to find a one-to-one mapping between points in the physical space

56:22.420 --> 56:26.760
to points in the image plane.

56:26.760 --> 56:31.400
This mapping is referred to as the perspective projection.

56:31.400 --> 56:36.640
Very important, as you'll see in a second.

56:36.640 --> 56:45.180
So assuming that this is how we define the reference frame, the answer is yes.

56:45.180 --> 56:50.640
So just to make a consistency check, so here, this x will be positive.

56:50.640 --> 56:54.480
Here the big Z will be negative, as you said.

56:54.480 --> 56:59.000
The x would also be negative, and then the two sign cancel out, so actually everything

56:59.000 --> 57:00.000
is.

57:00.360 --> 57:08.020
As I said, a little bit of symbols, but the concept here is very simple.

57:08.020 --> 57:09.020
Now this is great.

57:09.020 --> 57:12.100
So basically, now we know how to relate.

57:12.100 --> 57:16.700
So we know that we can capture images by using the notion of a p-hole, and we know how a

57:16.700 --> 57:22.180
point in the physical space is imaged in the image plane.

57:22.180 --> 57:24.940
So it seems that everything is set.

57:24.940 --> 57:32.600
Now we can start talking about how to reconstruct information and so on, but there is a caveat.

57:32.600 --> 57:39.840
The problem with the p-hole camera, as it is defined, is that if you have a very small

57:39.840 --> 57:46.320
aperture, then in the limit you only have one ray of light going through the hole.

57:46.320 --> 57:50.040
So you have, in principle, very crisp images.

57:50.040 --> 57:57.020
So here I'm showing a sequence of images that depend on the aperture of the p-hole.

57:57.020 --> 58:00.700
Two millimeters, one millimeter, 0.6, 0.35.

58:00.700 --> 58:06.620
As the radius of the p-hole decreases, the image becomes crisper and crisper, because

58:06.620 --> 58:11.380
we basically recover increasingly the one-to-one mapping that I referred before.

58:11.380 --> 58:16.860
The problem, though, is that as we make the radius smaller and smaller, an increasingly

58:16.860 --> 58:21.060
smaller amount of light goes through the p-hole.

58:21.060 --> 58:24.120
The point is that the image gets darker and darker.

58:24.120 --> 58:28.680
So in the limits that the radius gets smaller and smaller, you have a very little light

58:28.680 --> 58:33.620
that goes into your photoreceptive surface, so in principle you have a very crisp image.

58:33.620 --> 58:35.740
In practice, you don't see anything.

58:35.740 --> 58:40.300
Second of all, you approach the diffraction limit of light.

58:40.300 --> 58:45.260
So you might have other effects that perturb your image.

58:45.260 --> 58:47.140
So basically, you have a trade-off.

58:47.140 --> 58:52.300
On the one hand, you like to make the radius of the p-hole smaller and smaller, approaching

58:52.300 --> 58:53.900
zero, theoretically.

58:53.900 --> 58:59.500
On the other hand, you can't do it, because otherwise the image becomes very dark.

58:59.500 --> 59:03.100
And on top of that, you run into diffraction issues.

59:03.100 --> 59:04.400
How do you fix that?

59:04.400 --> 59:08.060
You fix it by introducing a notion of a lens.

59:08.060 --> 59:10.860
You had a question?

59:10.860 --> 59:11.860
Yep.

59:11.940 --> 59:20.740
So a lens is an optical element, in a nutshell, that focuses light by means of diffraction.

59:20.740 --> 59:30.500
So the idea is, instead of placing a plane with a p-hole, actually the idea is to place

59:30.500 --> 59:40.620
a lens that captures the rays of light coming out of a point and focuses all those rays

59:40.620 --> 59:43.420
into a single point in a photoreceptive surface.

59:43.420 --> 59:45.100
So the idea is the same as before.

59:45.100 --> 59:50.820
Try to come up with a one-to-one mapping between points on the photoreceptive surface and points

59:50.820 --> 59:53.500
in the physical world.

59:53.500 --> 59:55.260
But the way it works is different.

59:55.260 --> 59:59.700
Instead of forcing all the rays to go through the p-hole, or actually selecting only the

59:59.700 --> 01:00:03.980
ray that goes through the p-hole, the idea is to actually consider all the rays, but

01:00:03.980 --> 01:00:05.820
then focus them.

01:00:05.820 --> 01:00:11.220
In that way, you have the same one-to-one mapping, but now you can harness many more

01:00:11.220 --> 01:00:17.820
rays of light, which means that your image is much shinier.

01:00:17.820 --> 01:00:19.940
And any questions about this?

01:00:19.940 --> 01:00:27.180
So what we're trying to discuss now is the p-hole is conceptually appealing, but practically

01:00:27.180 --> 01:00:29.180
difficult to make it work.

01:00:29.180 --> 01:00:33.940
The idea is to introduce the concept of a lens in order to have something that approximates

01:00:33.940 --> 01:00:39.020
how a p-hole camera works, but at the same time is implementable.

01:00:39.020 --> 01:00:40.020
That's the whole idea.

01:00:40.020 --> 01:00:45.020
But doesn't this have the same problem as before, where you have other parts of the

01:00:45.020 --> 01:00:51.020
object that are also reflecting, also hitting the same parts of the lens, or all forms of

01:00:51.020 --> 01:00:52.020
the lens?

01:00:52.020 --> 01:00:57.760
Yeah, so each lens is capable of focusing images at a certain distance from a lens.

01:00:57.760 --> 01:01:00.900
And the geometry of this will come clear in a second.

01:01:00.900 --> 01:01:10.700
But not only that, but objects at a different distance from the lens may not focus perfectly

01:01:10.700 --> 01:01:11.700
on a given point.

01:01:11.700 --> 01:01:12.700
So it may look blurred.

01:01:12.700 --> 01:01:13.860
So that's a problem.

01:01:13.860 --> 01:01:17.500
In practice, there's not so much of a problem, as I will discuss next.

01:01:17.500 --> 01:01:20.440
But that's a very good point.

01:01:20.440 --> 01:01:25.060
So in order to answer the question that your colleague just asked, we had to understand

01:01:25.060 --> 01:01:32.580
a little bit better how points are projected on an image plane through a lens.

01:01:32.580 --> 01:01:39.220
The construction here is likely more complicated, but it's still relatively simple.

01:01:39.220 --> 01:01:40.280
So the idea is as follows.

01:01:40.280 --> 01:01:45.580
So we have a point in the physical space.

01:01:45.580 --> 01:01:52.700
We have a photoreceptive surface, and we have a lens.

01:01:52.700 --> 01:02:00.800
So the geometry of how points are mapped onto a photoreceptive surface obeys the three

01:02:00.800 --> 01:02:06.940
main laws that comes from this Snell law for light propagation through medium.

01:02:06.940 --> 01:02:08.560
We're not going to go into Snell law.

01:02:08.560 --> 01:02:16.340
So I'm just going to give you the results of the application of Snell laws to thin lenses.

01:02:16.340 --> 01:02:20.540
In particular, the rays passing through a lens obey three key properties.

01:02:20.580 --> 01:02:24.340
Again, they're not obvious, and we're not doing derivation right now.

01:02:24.340 --> 01:02:27.200
I just state these properties as facts.

01:02:27.200 --> 01:02:33.300
The first property is that rays that go through the center of a thin lens, a thin lens is

01:02:33.300 --> 01:02:38.780
a lens which is very thin, so the thickness of the lens is very small compared to the

01:02:38.780 --> 01:02:45.540
radius of curvature, a ray that goes through the center of the lens is not refracted.

01:02:45.540 --> 01:02:50.620
So for that specific ray that goes through the center of the lens, the geometry is the

01:02:50.620 --> 01:02:54.860
very same geometry of a pinhole camera.

01:02:54.860 --> 01:03:02.500
Rays that are parallel to the optical axis are focused on a point referred to as a focal

01:03:02.500 --> 01:03:05.300
point, F prime here.

01:03:05.300 --> 01:03:14.020
And third, all rays that are emitted by P are focused by the thin lens on a point P,

01:03:14.020 --> 01:03:19.980
so they intercept on a point P, which means that if you place the photoreceptive surface

01:03:19.980 --> 01:03:31.380
here, then you have a one-to-one mapping between capital P and this point here.

01:03:31.380 --> 01:03:40.580
So the question now is, how is the distance between this image plane and the plane that

01:03:40.580 --> 01:03:48.420
contains the lens related to the position of the object and the focal length?

01:03:48.420 --> 01:03:56.060
The key issue here is that as I change the position of the point capital P, the point

01:03:56.060 --> 01:03:59.340
at which the rays intersect would change.

01:03:59.340 --> 01:04:05.380
And that's annoying because if the point at which the image focuses differs for each point

01:04:05.380 --> 01:04:09.260
in the physical object, then where should you place the photoreceptive surface?

01:04:09.260 --> 01:04:11.860
Here or here, that's an issue.

01:04:11.860 --> 01:04:18.900
So what we're going to see is that for typical cameras, there is a range of distances for

01:04:18.900 --> 01:04:26.020
which the point, the image is in focus.

01:04:26.020 --> 01:04:30.020
And besides those range, then the image is actually out of focus or blurred.

01:04:30.020 --> 01:04:34.860
But for more typical applications, out of focus images are not so important.

01:04:34.860 --> 01:04:41.340
So let's first understand the relation between the distance from the lens at which a point

01:04:41.340 --> 01:04:47.580
becomes in focus, the position of capital P, and the geometry of the thin lens, in particular

01:04:47.580 --> 01:04:50.020
in terms of the focal length.

01:04:50.020 --> 01:04:56.020
In order to do so, we use the property of similar triangles.

01:04:56.020 --> 01:05:06.780
Two triangles are similar if the angles are equal, which means that the corresponding

01:05:06.780 --> 01:05:12.180
edges need to be in a proportionality factor.

01:05:12.180 --> 01:05:17.980
Well, more or less, I hope.

01:05:17.980 --> 01:05:24.740
So you see these angles are similar in that the, so these triangles are similar in that

01:05:24.780 --> 01:05:30.220
the corresponding angles are equal, which means that the corresponding edges, they have

01:05:30.220 --> 01:05:34.060
to be in a proportionality factor.

01:05:34.060 --> 01:05:38.780
So what triangles I'm going to consider here?

01:05:38.780 --> 01:05:46.020
I'm going to consider these two blue triangles and these two red triangles.

01:05:46.020 --> 01:05:48.700
Let's consider the blue one first.

01:05:48.700 --> 01:05:51.220
These two blue triangles are indeed similar.

01:05:51.620 --> 01:05:56.100
This angle is equal to this one, this angle is equal to this one, this one is equal to

01:05:56.100 --> 01:06:02.140
90 degrees, and this one is equal to this one, as you should easily see.

01:06:02.140 --> 01:06:05.580
Then we apply the property of similar triangles as follows.

01:06:05.580 --> 01:06:29.060
The ratio between this edge here, which will be small y, the y-coordinate of P, and this

01:06:29.060 --> 01:06:37.980
edge here, which will be big Y, so Y over Y over big Y, has to be equal to the ratio

01:06:37.980 --> 01:06:42.700
between the length of this edge and the length of this edge.

01:06:42.700 --> 01:06:51.820
This is a small z, this is a big Z.

01:06:51.820 --> 01:06:58.420
Then apply the same reasoning to the red triangles, which means that the ratio between this edge

01:06:58.420 --> 01:07:11.140
and this edge, so Y over big Y, has to be equal to the ratio between this edge and this

01:07:11.140 --> 01:07:12.420
edge here.

01:07:12.420 --> 01:07:17.280
So this edge is the focal length.

01:07:17.280 --> 01:07:20.740
So here we have the focal length.

01:07:20.740 --> 01:07:29.260
And this edge here is equal to the length of the overall edge minus the focal length.

01:07:29.260 --> 01:07:43.420
So this is Z minus F.

01:07:43.420 --> 01:07:48.880
So then we can insert this here.

01:07:48.880 --> 01:08:05.560
So we have that Z over capital Z is equal to Z over F minus one, which means by dividing

01:08:05.560 --> 01:08:15.160
by small z, that one over z is equal to one over F minus one over small z.

01:08:15.160 --> 01:08:21.340
Or in other words, we have this equation here, which is referred to as the thin length equation.

01:08:21.340 --> 01:08:24.100
So what this equation tells you?

01:08:24.100 --> 01:08:29.940
Let F be the focal length, which is a property of a thin length, something that you assume

01:08:29.940 --> 01:08:30.940
you know.

01:08:30.940 --> 01:08:37.720
Let Z be the distance of the point in the physical space from the length.

01:08:37.720 --> 01:08:45.280
And small z, the point at which the physical point becomes in focus, meaning that all the

01:08:45.280 --> 01:08:47.360
light rays intersect.

01:08:47.360 --> 01:08:52.880
And if you were to place a photoreceptive surface here, then the image would be in focus.

01:08:52.880 --> 01:08:55.880
That's the relation between these quantities.

01:08:56.820 --> 01:09:04.740
Interestingly, if an object is very far, so capital Z is much larger than the small z

01:09:04.740 --> 01:09:13.500
and small f, then objects that are very far away would focus on the length small z equal

01:09:13.500 --> 01:09:16.520
to the focal length.

01:09:16.520 --> 01:09:17.860
So what's the key point here?

01:09:17.860 --> 01:09:25.760
Is that in a camera, the z and the f are in general much smaller than a capital Z, which

01:09:25.760 --> 01:09:31.940
means that this approximation that objects are at infinity focus at the focal length

01:09:31.940 --> 01:09:34.340
is usually well verified.

01:09:34.340 --> 01:09:37.580
Which means that I can use my glasses and I'm not a blind person.

01:09:37.580 --> 01:09:50.880
So all of this to say that a thin lens is a very smart way of creating a device that

01:09:50.880 --> 01:09:54.100
works in a way that is very similar to a pinhole camera.

01:09:54.100 --> 01:09:58.000
And the reason why it works in a way that is very similar to the pinhole camera is through

01:09:58.000 --> 01:10:04.880
this thin lens equation that relates the position of a point in the physical space to where

01:10:04.880 --> 01:10:09.860
the point will be focused within the camera.

01:10:09.860 --> 01:10:16.260
In particular, the equations relating the position of capital P and small p are exactly

01:10:16.260 --> 01:10:21.920
the same under pinhole perspective if one considers small c as the focal length.

01:10:21.920 --> 01:10:26.780
This is clear because the geometry is exactly the same.

01:10:26.780 --> 01:10:31.780
And if capital Z goes to infinity, then a small z is equal to the focal length.

01:10:31.780 --> 01:10:38.180
So basically, we will have the same exact equations as before by using as f the focal

01:10:38.180 --> 01:10:43.300
length of the lens.

01:10:43.300 --> 01:10:47.140
Points located at a distance minus z from the origin and the length will be in sharp

01:10:47.140 --> 01:10:51.360
focus only when the image plane is located at a distance of small z from the origin or

01:10:51.380 --> 01:10:54.060
the other side of the lens that satisfies the thin lens equation.

01:10:54.060 --> 01:10:56.340
We already discussed this.

01:10:56.340 --> 01:11:00.580
In practice, objects within some range of distances called depth of field or depth of

01:11:00.580 --> 01:11:03.540
focus will be in acceptable focus.

01:11:03.540 --> 01:11:09.640
So if you have, for example, an object that is extremely close by, and that is the case,

01:11:09.640 --> 01:11:12.940
it will be difficult for me to put it in focus.

01:11:12.940 --> 01:11:20.460
There are more complicated lenses with multiple foci, but for normal lenses, that is not possible.

01:11:20.560 --> 01:11:24.880
Similarly, if capital Z goes to infinity, f becomes the distance between the center

01:11:24.880 --> 01:11:30.400
of the lens and the plane where distance objects focus.

01:11:30.400 --> 01:11:40.280
This idea allows us to treat cameras with lenses in the same way that we model a pinhole

01:11:40.280 --> 01:11:41.280
camera.

01:11:42.260 --> 01:11:50.740
For the rest of this class, what we will do will be to develop computer vision by using

01:11:50.740 --> 01:11:56.060
as a model a pinhole camera model with the understanding that in reality that model will

01:11:56.060 --> 01:11:59.940
be realized through the notion of a lens.

01:11:59.940 --> 01:12:06.420
But thanks to this correspondence between cameras with lenses and pinhole cameras, we

01:12:06.420 --> 01:12:10.360
can accurately talk about the two and it's just mathematically more convenient to talk

01:12:10.420 --> 01:12:12.420
about pinhole cameras.

01:12:12.420 --> 01:12:18.020
So, a couple more things before we leave for today.

01:12:18.020 --> 01:12:22.820
So now that I hope you agree with me that we can, even though every camera has a lens

01:12:22.820 --> 01:12:28.000
is not a pinhole camera, we can use a pinhole camera model to model the process whereby

01:12:28.000 --> 01:12:29.720
we gather images.

01:12:29.720 --> 01:12:37.660
Now the first task that we want to understand is how a point in the physical space is mapped

01:12:37.660 --> 01:12:40.680
into the image plane.

01:12:40.680 --> 01:12:45.160
And in particular, this entails three steps.

01:12:45.160 --> 01:12:52.120
First of all, we want to understand how a point, which is expressed in terms of coordinates

01:12:52.120 --> 01:12:58.380
with respect to the camera reference system, is mapped into the image plane.

01:12:58.380 --> 01:13:04.320
Then we want to understand how that coordinates can be mapped into pixel coordinates.

01:13:04.320 --> 01:13:11.920
So that we can actually measure points in pixels, not in continuous distances.

01:13:11.920 --> 01:13:18.900
And finally, we want to understand the relation between the point in the physical space as

01:13:18.900 --> 01:13:24.360
expressed in the camera reference system to the same point as expressed in a word inertial

01:13:24.360 --> 01:13:25.820
reference system.

01:13:25.820 --> 01:13:30.440
So that once we do that, we have a complete end-to-end mapping between the point that

01:13:30.440 --> 01:13:33.520
is expressed in the word reference frame.

01:13:33.520 --> 01:13:36.400
Pw means word, inertial reference frame.

01:13:36.400 --> 01:13:38.380
C means the camera reference system.

01:13:38.380 --> 01:13:44.080
We have a mapping from Pw all the way to P in pixels, again, built into three phases.

01:13:44.080 --> 01:13:53.560
We go from a Pw to Pc, we go from Pc to P, and then we go from P to pixels.

01:13:53.560 --> 01:13:55.680
Is the procedure clear?

01:13:55.680 --> 01:13:57.140
All right.

01:13:57.140 --> 01:14:02.720
So the first step is assume that we start from a point whose coordinates are given the

01:14:02.720 --> 01:14:04.720
camera reference system.

01:14:04.720 --> 01:14:10.720
How do we get the coordinates for the projected point in the image plane?

01:14:10.720 --> 01:14:13.000
We already seen that.

01:14:13.000 --> 01:14:17.800
And in particular, x is equal to the focal length xc over cc, y is equal to f, yc over

01:14:17.800 --> 01:14:18.800
cc.

01:14:18.800 --> 01:14:24.960
Here, I just made explicit that x, y, and z are with respect to the camera reference

01:14:24.960 --> 01:14:30.640
system.

01:14:30.640 --> 01:14:37.160
The second step is, OK, so we have the point in the image plane with respect to the camera

01:14:37.160 --> 01:14:38.760
reference system.

01:14:38.760 --> 01:14:47.360
How do those coordinates relate to the coordinates that a camera really uses, which are basically

01:14:47.360 --> 01:14:49.800
pixels?

01:14:49.800 --> 01:14:52.280
So I don't want to rush it, so I will do it next time.

01:14:52.280 --> 01:14:57.640
But basically, the idea is simply to translate a continuous quantity into discretized quantities

01:14:57.640 --> 01:14:59.000
through pixels.

01:14:59.000 --> 01:15:02.160
So that's something that we will do next time.

01:15:02.160 --> 01:15:06.440
And finally, we will have the final step that we will do next time about how to go from

01:15:06.440 --> 01:15:08.820
a PW to PC.

01:15:08.820 --> 01:15:14.840
The only difficulty in doing all of this will be that, as we will see, where it's also clear

01:15:14.840 --> 01:15:19.760
from here, that this mapping entails a division by zc.

01:15:19.760 --> 01:15:21.800
So this mapping is nonlinear.

01:15:21.800 --> 01:15:25.240
And dealing with the nonlinear max-links in general is difficult.

01:15:25.240 --> 01:15:30.840
So we'll find a trick referred to as homogeneous coordinates that allow us to represent this

01:15:30.840 --> 01:15:33.520
mapping as a linear one.

01:15:33.520 --> 01:15:38.280
Once we do that, we'll be able to talk about camera calibration.

01:15:38.280 --> 01:15:44.660
So how do we infer all the intrinsic parameters of a camera, such as the focal length?

01:15:44.660 --> 01:15:49.200
And at that point, we'll talk a bit about how to then reconstruct the scene by measuring

01:15:49.200 --> 01:15:51.920
distances to objects by using images.

01:15:51.920 --> 01:15:54.200
And that will be the lecture on Thursday.

01:15:54.200 --> 01:15:59.600
So we just had four or five slides left, but I don't want to rush them.

01:15:59.600 --> 01:16:03.720
So we will start from here on Thursday.

01:16:03.720 --> 01:16:06.800
And then we will move into the true lecture of Thursday, which is about camera calibration.

